41	/**
42	 * Simple Log for wake lock events. Optimized to reduce memory usage.
43	 *
44	 * The wake lock events are ultimately saved in-memory in a pre-allocated byte-based ring-buffer.
45	 *
46	 * Most of the work of this log happens in the {@link BackgroundThread}.
47	 *
48	 * The main log is basically just a sequence of the two wake lock events (ACQUIRE and RELEASE).
49	 * Each entry in the log stores the following data:
50	 *  {
51	 *    event type (RELEASE | ACQUIRE),
52	 *    time (64-bit from System.currentTimeMillis()),
53	 *    wake-lock ID {ownerUID (int) + tag (String)},
54	 *    wake-lock flags
55	 *  }
56	 *
57	 * In order to maximize the number of entries that fit into the log, there are various efforts made
58	 * to compress what we store; of which two are fairly significant and contribute the most to the
59	 * complexity of this code:
60	 * A) Relative Time
61	 *     - Time in each log entry is stored as an 8-bit value and is relative to the time of the
62	 *       previous event. When relative time is too large for 8-bits, we add a third type of event
63	 *       called TIME_RESET, which is used to add a new 64-bit reference-time event to the log.
64	 *       In practice, TIME_RESETs seem to make up about 10% or less of the total events depending
65	 *       on the device usage.
66	 * B) Wake-lock tag/ID as indexes
67	 *     - Wake locks are often reused many times. To avoid storing large strings in the ring buffer,
68	 *       we maintain a {@link TagDatabase} that associates each wakelock tag with an 7-bit index.
69	 *       The main log stores only these 7-bit indexes instead of whole strings.
70	 *
71	 * To make the code a bit more organized, there exists a class {@link EntryByteTranslator} which
72	 * uses the tag database, and reference-times to convert between a {@link LogEntry} and the
73	 * byte sequence that is ultimately stored in the main log, {@link TheLog}.
